      "question": "<p>Given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta\"></span> is measured in radians, prove, from first principles, that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm d\\theta}(\\cos\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>You may assume the formula for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos(A\\pm B)\"></span> and that as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\\to 0\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{\\sin h}{h}\\to 1\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{\\cos h-1}{h}\\to 0\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
